ROLE: You add one exported function to a TypeScript module.

CONTEXT: `.skilled/skills/sk-vision/vision-runtime/src/opencode/tools.ts` exports `skVisionTools(provider, opts)`, which returns a map of 13 OpenCode 1 tool definitions built with `tool()` from `@opencode-ai/plugin`. Each one is `{ description, args, execute(args, context) }`: `args` is a zod raw shape built from `tool.schema`, and `execute` resolves to a string or to `{ output, ... }`. OpenCode 2 registers a tool through an editor whose `add()` takes `{ name, description, input, execute(input) }`, where `input` is a schema and `execute` resolves to `{ content }`.

ACTION: Re-read the file. Insert exactly this text directly after the closing `}` of the `skVisionTools` function (the line after `  } as const;`), separated from it by one blank line, and before `export function parseBBox`:

/** A tool in the shape OpenCode 2's tool editor accepts. */
export interface V2ToolDefinition {
  readonly name: string;
  readonly description: string;
  readonly input: unknown;
  readonly execute: (input: unknown) => Promise<{ readonly content: string }>;
}

/**
 * Re-shape the OpenCode 1 tool map for OpenCode 2's tool editor. The input
 * schema comes from the same zod copy the arguments were built with, so
 * OpenCode 2 parses a call's arguments the way OpenCode 1 does. The tools never
 * read the call context, so none is passed.
 */
export function toV2Tools(tools: ReturnType<typeof skVisionTools>): V2ToolDefinition[] {
  return Object.entries(tools).map(([name, definition]) => ({
    name,
    description: definition.description,
    input: tool.schema.object(definition.args),
    execute: async (input) => {
      const result = await definition.execute(input as never, undefined as never);
      return { content: typeof result === "string" ? result : result.output };
    },
  }));
}

Change nothing else.

FORMAT: Then run `cd .skilled/skills/sk-vision/vision-runtime && bunx tsc --noEmit; echo typecheck-exit=$?` and print its output verbatim, nothing else.
